{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The panel of trained non-experts will produce more reliable judgments than the single expert, but only if the checklist encodes genuine epistemic rules; if it functions merely as a procedural scaffold structuring deliberation, its reliability gain is real but non-epistemic and will fail to transfer beyond items matching its rule content.","counterconditions":["The corrupted-checklist panel (whose rule content is wrong but whose procedural structure is preserved) still outperforms the single expert, indicating the checklist's contribution is procedural rather than epistemic.","The divergence stratum (cases where checklist rule conflicts with expert intuition) is empty or too small for statistical inference, making it impossible to isolate the checklist's causal contribution within a single evaluation.","The panel outperforms the expert only on cases where checklist and intuition agree (the compliance stratum), with no outperformance on divergence cases, indicating the gain is noise reduction rather than rule-mediated epistemic leverage."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"The panel's reliability advantage arises from two separable channels: (1) noise reduction via aggregation of independent judgments, which is content-independent and would occur even under a corrupted checklist; and (2) epistemic leverage from checklist rules, which is content-dependent and only manifests on divergence cases where the checklist's rule conflicts with intuitive judgment. If the checklist is a perfect codification of expertise, channel (2) is indistinguishable from channel (1) because there is no divergence stratum to isolate the rule's causal contribution. To distinguish genuine epistemic gain from mere compliance, one must examine whether the panel outperforms the expert specifically on cases where checklist rules override intuition — the divergence stratum — rather than merely on cases where checklist and intuition agree.","optional_refs":[],